    <div class="game_name">
        <div class="game_title">
            <h3>chess</h3>
            <p>
                Chess is a two-player strategy board game that is played on an 8x8 grid known as a chessboard. It is one
                of
                the most popular and enduring board games in the world, known for its rich history and complex gameplay.
                Here's some brief information about chess:
                Objective: The primary objective of chess is to checkmate your opponent's king. Checkmate occurs when
                the
                opponent's king is in a position to be captured (in check), and there is no legal move that can remove
                the
                threat of capture.

                <br> <b> Chess Pieces:</b> Each player starts with 16 pieces, including one king, one queen, two rooks,
                two
                knights, two
                bishops, and eight pawns. Each piece has its own unique way of moving across the board.

                <br> <b>Movement:</b> Chess pieces move in distinct patterns. For example, the queen can move diagonally
                and
                in straight
                lines, while the knight has a unique L-shaped move. The king can move one square in any direction.

                <br> <b>Special Moves:</b> En passant and castling are two special moves in chess. En passant allows a
                pawn
                to capture
                an opponent's pawn that has just moved two squares forward. Castling is a move involving the king and a
                rook
                to improve the king's safety.

                <br> <b> Chess Notation:</b> Chess games are often recorded using a standard notation system, such as
                Algebraic Notation,
                to keep track of moves and strategies.

                <br> <b> Endgame: </b>The endgame is the final phase of a chess game where there are fewer pieces on the
                board. It
                requires precise technique and calculation to convert an advantage into a win or defend to achieve a
                draw.

                <br> <b> Chess Variants:</b>Over the years, many variants of chess have been created, such as speed
                chess,
                Fischer
                Random Chess (Chess960), and more, each with its own rules and unique characteristics.

                <br> <b> International Competitions:</b> Chess is played at the highest levels in international
                competitions, including
                the World Chess Championship, where the world's top players compete for the title of world champion.
            </p>
        </div>

