        <div class="game_title are1">
            <h3> BRIDGE</h3>
            Bridge is a popular card game that is typically played by four players in two partnerships. It is known for
            its complexity, strategic depth, and social aspects. Here's a brief overview of the game:

            <br> <b> Objective: </b>The primary objective of Bridge is to score points by winning tricks (rounds of
            cards played) and
            fulfilling contracts that specify the number of tricks a partnership must take.

            <br> <b> Card Deck:</b> Bridge is played with a standard deck of 52 cards. The ranking of the cards, from
            highest to
            lowest, is Ace, King, Queen, Jack, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2.

            <br> <b>Partnerships:</b> Four players form two partnerships, sitting opposite each other. Partnerships are
            often
            referred to as North-South and East-West.

            <br> <b> Bidding: </b> The game begins with a bidding phase, during which players bid to declare a
            "contract." The
            contract specifies the number of tricks a partnership must take and the trump suit (or "no trump"). Bidding
            involves communication between partners to determine the best contract based on their hands.

            <br> <b> Play:</b> After the bidding phase, the player who won the contract becomes the declarer. The
            declarer's partner
            is the dummy, and the opposing partnership becomes the defenders. The declarer tries to win as many tricks
            as specified in the contract, while the defenders aim to prevent the declarer from doing so.

            <br> <b> Scoring:</b> Bridge uses a point-based scoring system. Points can be earned for fulfilling
            contracts, overtricks
            (taking more tricks than contracted), and undertricks (failing to fulfill the contract). There are also
            bonuses for achieving certain milestones, such as bidding and making a "game" or a "slam."

            <br> <b>Strategy:</b> Bridge is a highly strategic game that involves careful planning, communication, and
            a deep
            understanding of the game's conventions and tactics. Players use signals and bidding systems to convey
            information about their hands to their partners.

            <br> <b> Variations: </b>There are several variations of Bridge, including Rubber Bridge and Duplicate
            Bridge. Duplicate
            Bridge is a competitive form where the same deals are played by different partnerships, emphasizing skill
            rather than luck.


            <br><b> Competitive Play:</b> Competitive Bridge is organized into clubs and tournaments, with players
            earning ranking
            points and titles. The World Bridge Federation (WBF) governs international competitive play.

            <br> Bridge is a game that combines elements of skill, strategy, psychology, and teamwork. It has a
            dedicated
            following of players worldwide and is often enjoyed by people of all ages.
        </div>
    </div>

    {{!-- rubix cube --}}

    <div class="game_name">
        <div class="game_title">
            <h3>Rubik’s Cube</h3>

            The Rubik's Cube, often referred to simply as the "Rubik's Cube" or "Rubix Cube," is a three-dimensional
            mechanical puzzle and brain-teaser. Here's a brief overview:

            <br> <b>Invention: </b>The Rubik's Cube was invented in 1974 by Ernő Rubik, a Hungarian professor of
            architecture.
            Originally, he called it the "Magic Cube."

            <br> <b> Design:</b> The cube consists of 6 faces, each made up of 9 smaller squares of the same color. The
            faces can
            be rotated independently in any direction, which creates a complex puzzle with numerous possible
            combinations.

            <br> <b> Objective:</b> The goal of the Rubik's Cube is to solve it by returning each face to a single,
            solid color.
            To do this, you must manipulate the cube's layers to align all the smaller squares by color.

            <br> <b> Difficulty:</b> The Rubik's Cube is known for its challenging nature. There are
            43,252,003,274,489,856,000
            possible combinations, but only one solution. Solving it requires logical thinking, pattern recognition,
            and a series of algorithms.

            <br> <b> Competitions:</b> Speedcubing is a competitive sport where participants aim to solve the Rubik's
            Cube as
            quickly as possible. There are official World Cube Association (WCA) competitions held globally for
            speedcubing enthusiasts.

            <br> <b> Variants:</b> Over the years, various Rubik's Cube variants and puzzles have been created,
            including the
            2x2x2 Pocket Cube, the 4x4x4 Rubik's Revenge, and the 5x5x5 Professor's Cube, among others.

            <br> <B> Popularity:</B> The Rubik's Cube gained widespread popularity in the 1980s and has remained a
            classic puzzle
            and iconic toy ever since. It's considered one of the best-selling toys of all time.

            <br> <b> Solving Methods:</b> Several solving methods and algorithms have been developed, with the most
            famous being
            the Fridrich Method, CFOP (Cross, F2L, OLL, PLL), which is used by many speedcubers.

            The Rubik's Cube has become a symbol of problem-solving and intellectual challenge and continues to
            captivate people of all ages around the world. It's not only a fun puzzle but also a test of patience
            and perseverance.


        </div>

